00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.520
are only possible if you
have the right tools.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.800
So that's why today I want
to talk to you about how

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.870
you can put together a serving
infrastructure with the managed

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:24.530
infrastructure features and
get your software deployed

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.686
onto it nicely and smoothly.

00:01:28.686 --> 00:01:30.560
And I built this
presentation both for people

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:33.950
who are kind of new to GCE
and those who are using GCE

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:36.230
but aren't using the
managed pieces yet.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.630
And if you are a managed
instance group expert, then

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.790
hopefully you'll still
get some value out

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:44.310
of some of the new features
that we'll be showing off.

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:45.430
So let's start small.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:50.710
If you want to build a
service from scratch,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.860
you might start with
one server and you just

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.370
provision a virtual machine.

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:58.540
And you might have given it
some sort of a clever name.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:01.730
And then to deploy, you just
log in and deploy your software.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:03.730
And when there is an
update, it's not a problem.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:05.440
You just log in
again and update it.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.710
And then that's fine.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:12.440
But even when you have maybe two
of the servers or three, five,

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:14.240
or 10, you can
probably still manage

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.550
because you can come
up with 10 clever names

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.200
and log into each one of them
and deploy your software.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:19.940
And that's OK.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.690
It's getting a little bit
painful, but you can manage.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.450
But when you start getting
into hundreds, thousands,

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.750
or tens of thousands of
servers, this manual approach

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.400
really doesn't work anymore.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.000
And that's when you might have
switched to some sort of a CICD

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.670
system where suddenly
your servers are

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.390
these nameless, mindless
drones and they get spun up

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:42.469
by other systems or by software.

00:02:42.469 --> 00:02:44.510
And now you don't have to
worry about naming them

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.015
and they just get created.

00:02:46.015 --> 00:02:47.240
And that's really good.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:53.750
But, when one of them goes
down, you have to do something.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.210
Someone has to log in-- and
maybe it is actually literally

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:56.990
you--

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:59.960
to now restart this
machine, recreate it.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:01.640
You have to take an action.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.150
Or if traffic dips, you
suddenly have all these servers

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:06.352
that are idle.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.060
And if you had a bad
job and it finished,

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:11.290
maybe all of the
servers are idle.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.910
And that's why a
managed instance group

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:18.890
is this agent that monitors
your servers for you

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:22.900
and takes action on your
behalf to do the right thing.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:23.900
Now that's really vague.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.840
So let's break it down.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.050
Manage instance group
has four major features.

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.390
One, there is the autoscaler.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.630
So that's the thing that people
typically start with for MIGs.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:38.870
And the autoscaler can
create and destroy machines

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:40.469
for you based on load.

00:03:40.469 --> 00:03:42.260
And you can define what
load means for you,

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:46.670
whether that's CPU utilization
or traffic or, with custom

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:49.600
metrics, something
completely different.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.460
There's the autohealer which
will recreate instances that

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.514
become unhealthy and go down.

00:03:54.514 --> 00:03:56.180
And again, you can
define what unhealthy

00:03:56.180 --> 00:04:00.770
means for your service
with custom health checks.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:03.890
There is now one of the new
features, the updater, which

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:06.590
is currently in alpha,
going to beta very shortly,

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:08.960
which will help you deploy
software to your managed

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.840
instance group in a controlled
manner in exactly the way

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.740
you kind of need it and
what works for your service.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.769
And there is regional, which
will split your machines

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:22.100
across different zones to make
your service more available

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.337
or move you towards
high availability.

00:04:27.676 --> 00:04:30.372
So that's the four pieces.

00:04:30.372 --> 00:04:32.830
And the one thing to keep in
mind with the managed instance

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:35.560
group is that all of the
pieces are integrated

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:37.540
and they work well
together, which is great.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:39.550
But more importantly,
they're also

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:42.357
modular, which means that if
you don't need autoscaling,

00:04:42.357 --> 00:04:43.815
you actually don't
have to take it.

00:04:43.815 --> 00:04:46.570
Managed instance groups
are more than just groups

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:48.370
of scaling machines.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:50.620
If you have a static
work set, then you

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.590
can have a MIG that maybe just
uses autohealing and updates,

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:55.510
and it doesn't even
use autoscaling.

00:04:55.510 --> 00:05:00.970
Or maybe you have a zonal split
with regional and updates,

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:02.980
but you don't need autohealing.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.230
And we'll dig into some of
these features in detail

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.756
throughout the presentation.

00:05:07.756 --> 00:05:09.130
So now let's talk
about software.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:12.970
Or more specifically, let's talk
about the software lifecycle.

00:05:12.970 --> 00:05:15.100
And for purposes of
this, let's break it

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.460
down to something super simple.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.051
You write.

00:05:18.051 --> 00:05:18.550
You build.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:19.270
You test.

00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:20.290
And you deploy.

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:22.420
And because it's a
cycle, you do this

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.710
over and over and over again.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:26.470
So let's say you
built some software,

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:29.230
and you decided to package
it into a container,

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:32.610
because containers are
cool and stackable,

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:34.380
one of these containers.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.206
And now you have
your container pack.

00:05:37.206 --> 00:05:39.580
And now you're thinking, I
want to get it into the cloud.

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:41.860
Well it turns out there
are a lot of options

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:45.280
for how you actually can deploy
your container into the cloud.

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:47.980
On one end of the spectrum,
you can go bare metal

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.942
and you can provision
your own machine.

00:05:50.942 --> 00:05:51.900
You can install Docker.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:52.851
You can configure it.

00:05:52.851 --> 00:05:54.100
You can install the container.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.140
You can configure
networking and you basically

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:58.144
do all of this yourself,
and that works OK.

00:05:58.144 --> 00:05:59.560
On the other end
of the scale, you

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:01.600
can go with the
Container Engine.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.480
And you can ask us to revisit
provision a cluster for you.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:06.010
We'll manage the
cluster for you.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:07.551
You then just upload
your containers.

00:06:07.551 --> 00:06:09.270
And that works OK as well.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:12.779
In between the two there is
Kubernetes, where you basically

00:06:12.779 --> 00:06:14.320
take care of your
own cluster and you

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:15.495
install Kubernetes on it.

00:06:15.495 --> 00:06:16.870
And then Kubernetes
does the kind

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.480
of scheduling of the containers.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:22.150
And now we're introducing
this other option

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:23.920
that is essentially
UI passthrough where

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.300
if all you need is to put
one container on each machine

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:31.987
and you want some
automation with that, well

00:06:31.987 --> 00:06:33.570
we'll take care of
some of the details

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:36.111
so that you don't have to worry
about setting it up yourself.

00:06:36.111 --> 00:06:38.700
So let me explain to
you what that means.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.040
You still start by
building your container

00:06:41.040 --> 00:06:44.640
and then uploading it to the
container repository as normal.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.210
And then in the interface, when
you go to create a new machine,

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:51.780
there's a tick box which says
that I want this instance to be

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.040
containerized.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.470
And then you just pass
a link to the container,

00:06:55.470 --> 00:06:58.647
open up whatever ports you
need, give it to command line,

00:06:58.647 --> 00:06:59.980
and we'll take care of the rest.

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:01.563
And what do we do
in the background is

00:07:01.563 --> 00:07:06.870
we'll provision the machine,
Grab the image that has Docker

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.870
already preconfigured on it--
it's the Google official Docker

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:13.470
image, put the container on
it, run the command line,

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:15.900
set up the networking
the way you open it up.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:17.310
And that's kind of it.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.260
So for that really
straightforward case

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:21.810
where you just wrapped your
software into a container,

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:23.080
this could be an option.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.520
So let's take a look at what
that looks like in practice

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.350
with the new interface.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:30.000
So you start as
you normally would

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:32.790
with creating an instance.

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:36.570
And let's give it a clever
name so that we can potentially

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:37.770
log into it later on.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.230
Put it into us-central.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:50.330
And here's the new
passthrough where

00:07:50.330 --> 00:07:52.430
you can say, OK,
well, now I want

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:53.650
you to load this container.

00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:56.130
Go over to the container
repository, grab a link to it.

00:08:08.334 --> 00:08:10.250
And because this is a
really simple front end,

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:11.570
let's open up port 80.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:18.054
And now we have to tell
the container launcher

00:08:18.054 --> 00:08:19.220
how to launch the container.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:21.790
So it's a script
written in Python.

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:25.840
So let's launch Python
with front end hc.py.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:26.740
And done.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:27.730
And that's it.

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:29.620
You can see those containerized
with that little summary

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:30.119
at the top.

00:08:40.136 --> 00:08:41.260
The machine is spinning up.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:43.727
And soon as it gets
provisioned, what will happen

00:08:43.727 --> 00:08:45.310
is in the background,
as I said, we'll

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:48.840
basically load the container
on and run the command

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.180
that you passed us.

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:10.340
OK, the machine has
spun up and ready to go.

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:11.960
And when you go into
the machine then,

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:13.930
you can see that
it's containerized.

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:16.190
In the details, there's
a link to the container

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:19.060
that was preloaded on it.

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:22.599
And if we go to the actual
IP address for the machine--

00:09:22.599 --> 00:09:23.765
give it a second to refresh.

00:09:27.382 --> 00:09:28.090
And there you go.

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:33.560
It's serving the super simple
front end, healthy true,

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:37.042
and deployment done.

00:09:37.042 --> 00:09:39.000
So that worked really
well for that simple case

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:39.666
we talked about.

00:09:39.666 --> 00:09:42.097
We have one machine,
and it's set up.

00:09:42.097 --> 00:09:44.430
And it loaded the container
with basically just a little

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:45.540
passthrough, great.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:50.650
Now if we have a
managed instance group,

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:53.695
we want to be able to
create multiple machines,

00:09:53.695 --> 00:09:55.570
because that one machine
might not be enough.

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:58.787
So maybe you need two,
three, 10, 15 of them.

00:09:58.787 --> 00:10:01.120
But before you can create the
group, what you need to do

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.850
is describe how to
create these machines.

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:09.050
So the MIG needs a recipe or
a template, which will explain

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:11.424
or which will allow it to
provision machines for you.

00:10:11.424 --> 00:10:13.090
Because the whole
point is that now it's

00:10:13.090 --> 00:10:18.700
the MIG that will be creating
the machines, not you manually.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.250
And when the template
is set up, the MIG

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:23.300
can be creating the machines.

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:25.360
So now let's take a
look at setting up

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:28.314
a containerized
managed instance group.

00:10:28.314 --> 00:10:29.980
So same thing we did
with the instances,

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:33.340
but now we're going to
go with a group of them.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:36.730
So we start with
creating a template.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:39.490
And of course the interface
for creating a template

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:42.010
is very similar to the interface
for creating an instance,

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:43.874
because it's basically
a description of how

00:10:43.874 --> 00:10:45.040
to put together an instance.

00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:50.011
So you can see the same check
box to grab your container.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.720
And we'll grab the same
version, open up the port 80.

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:12.930
And run that same
Python front end script.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:28.184
Allow the firewall passthrough
for HTTP, and create.

00:11:28.184 --> 00:11:30.600
And of course, the difference
is that when you hit Create,

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:32.990
nothing actually get created
in terms of instances.

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:35.700
It's just the template that
was created, so the descriptor.

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:37.490
And from the template
now we can say,

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:39.790
OK, I want you now to
create multiple instances.

00:11:39.790 --> 00:11:42.120
So create a group
of these for me.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:46.500
And so this is a second flow
into how to create groups.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:49.740
The other main flow is to
create a group from the Instance

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.150
Groups tab on the left side.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:54.730
But you can also create a
group directly from a template.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:56.590
So now we'll give
our group a name.

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:59.370
And we'll call it next
containerized instance group

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:00.860
one.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:05.010
Leave the single zone and
choose same us-central.

00:12:08.246 --> 00:12:09.620
The template is
already selected.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:12.750
And now we're going to
go with 10 instances.

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:22.870
Hit Create, and
we're good to go.

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:24.910
So now the group itself
is being created.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.550
And as soon as it
becomes a resource,

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:28.129
we can hook it up
to a load balancer.

00:12:28.129 --> 00:12:29.670
Because now that we
have 10 machines,

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:32.160
obviously we want to
have a single IP that

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:35.370
can be accessed by the users.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:38.040
And so we don't want to have
10 individual instances.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.817
So the group is being created.

00:12:40.817 --> 00:12:42.150
The instances are being created.

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:49.300
And now we can tap over to
Networking and Load Balancer,

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:50.950
and choose one of
the load balancers

00:12:50.950 --> 00:12:52.570
that was created previously.

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:54.435
But it's empty as in,
there is no back end.

00:12:54.435 --> 00:12:55.810
So right now that
little balancer

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:58.000
has no target to
send traffic to.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:02.040
And we can now, from a dropdown,
select the new managed instance

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:05.020
we just created, next
containerized IG1.

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:06.820
Hit Update.

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:10.324
And networking
will get connected.

00:13:10.324 --> 00:13:12.240
Just make sure that the
instances are created.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:16.270
So you can check each individual
instance, whether it's serving.

00:13:16.270 --> 00:13:17.820
And yes, yes it is.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:23.095
If we go direct, I'm going to
go back to our load balanced IP.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:33.992
And it takes a second or three
to connect through networking.

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:49.040
And there it is.

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:50.816
And there you go.

00:13:50.816 --> 00:13:52.940
So now the traffic that
went to the load balance IP

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:56.000
got passed through to one of
the containerized instances.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:59.190
And it all works just
like we kind of expected.

00:13:59.190 --> 00:13:59.690
Great.

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:02.720
So that worked really
nicely for that setup.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:04.650
We have one machine
that we scale up to 10.

00:14:04.650 --> 00:14:07.440
We can connect it
to a load balancer.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:08.750
Everything's good.

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:11.720
But now our service is
a little bit exposed

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:12.890
to potentially going down.

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:14.960
And there are typically
two things that

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:16.540
can bring your service down.

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:19.730
So one, there is that
concept of a zonal outage

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:22.670
where all of these instances
we put into that one

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:25.220
us-central c1, I think, zone.

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:27.017
And if that zone goes
down or has an outage

00:14:27.017 --> 00:14:29.350
or has some networking problems,
then your whole service

00:14:29.350 --> 00:14:30.560
is down.

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:33.170
And then two, which is
quite frankly more likely,

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:35.409
is your software might have
an issue or the machine

00:14:35.409 --> 00:14:37.450
might start crashing and
your service will slowly

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:40.880
start experiencing machine
by machine outages.

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:43.370
And that's what
autohealing can help with.

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:45.650
So now that you're in this
managed instance group,

00:14:45.650 --> 00:14:47.490
you can easily deal
with both cases.

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:50.530
So let's take a look at
the zonal problem first.

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:53.420
Instead of a regular MIG, you
can create regional managed

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:55.220
instance group.

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:57.410
The main difference is
that the regional MIG

00:14:57.410 --> 00:15:01.070
will split your instances into
zones, proportionally, and make

00:15:01.070 --> 00:15:02.630
sure that they're
always balanced

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:04.450
in terms of their
number of instances

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:06.050
as you're scaling up and down.

00:15:06.050 --> 00:15:07.633
So you don't have
to worry about doing

00:15:07.633 --> 00:15:10.640
that kind of balancing yourself
across the different zones.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:13.580
You just pick a region
and we'll make sure

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:18.410
that you have the right
amount of instances per zone.

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:21.300
So if there's an outage,
you're protected.

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:23.045
But there's a bit
more detail to this.

00:15:23.045 --> 00:15:24.170
So let's imagine this case.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:25.190
You have three zones.

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:27.242
You have your 10 instances
and they're serving

00:15:27.242 --> 00:15:27.950
and you're happy.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:29.822
And then zone A goes down.

00:15:29.822 --> 00:15:31.280
Well now those
three instances have

00:15:31.280 --> 00:15:33.890
to be reallocated
to the other zones.

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:35.979
But if a zone
really did go down,

00:15:35.979 --> 00:15:37.520
you might have a
lot of people trying

00:15:37.520 --> 00:15:40.610
to do the very same thing, which
means that at the time when you

00:15:40.610 --> 00:15:43.910
need it most, you might be
not getting any of instances

00:15:43.910 --> 00:15:45.660
because of stockouts.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:48.350
So that's why the best
practice we advise

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:51.920
is to actually overprovision
or create additional machines

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:55.150
that you don't strictly need to
serve the traffic you have now,

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:58.460
but that you can use
when there is an outage.

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:01.580
And the best practice is
to provision basically

00:16:01.580 --> 00:16:04.220
50% more machines per zone.

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:05.690
Now why 50%?

00:16:05.690 --> 00:16:07.940
If you have this case and
you've got your three zones,

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:10.110
and you have 10 machines
that are serving

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:12.796
and you have some extras,
if zone A goes out,

00:16:12.796 --> 00:16:14.420
those three machines
have now somewhere

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:17.960
to failover into or
have the traffic be

00:16:17.960 --> 00:16:20.040
served by other
machines, which is great.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:22.860
But of course the machines
you overprovisioned in zone A

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:23.907
are now offline as well.

00:16:23.907 --> 00:16:25.490
So that's why you
kind of have to have

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:28.865
more in the other zones.

00:16:28.865 --> 00:16:31.240
So that works really well for
that kind of zonal outages.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:35.200
And now you're more highly
available, which is great.

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:39.420
But for certain use cases,
even that might not be enough.

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:42.750
And you might start looking at
cross regional availability,

00:16:42.750 --> 00:16:44.620
which is out of the
scope of this talk.

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:47.700
But there is a really good
talk that happened yesterday,

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:51.349
IO204, designing
reliable systems

00:16:51.349 --> 00:16:52.890
with cloud
infrastructure, which goes

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:54.890
into great detail of kind
of global availability

00:16:54.890 --> 00:16:56.474
and how to think about that.

00:16:56.474 --> 00:16:58.140
So check it out if
you want to know more

00:16:58.140 --> 00:17:01.790
about how to protect
yourself from outages.

00:17:01.790 --> 00:17:04.200
OK, so that was the zonal part.

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:06.880
But I did mention that
it's more likely, actually,

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:09.550
there might be software
machine issues.

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:13.579
So let's look at autohealing
and health checks.

00:17:13.579 --> 00:17:16.890
Now health check is really
just a simple method

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:19.680
of determining whether
your machine is healthy.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:21.240
That's all it is,
which typically

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:24.240
means you ping it on some
path with a certain frequency.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:26.310
And at some point you
determine that a machine

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:30.390
is not healthy or needs
to be healed or restarted

00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:31.500
or something.

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:37.290
And both load balancer and the
autohealer use health checks.

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:40.560
Now there's an important
thing to keep in mind.

00:17:40.560 --> 00:17:42.744
These services actually
have a very different use

00:17:42.744 --> 00:17:43.660
for the health checks.

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:47.970
So while they are both health
checks, when a load balancer is

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:50.370
using a health check
and one of the machines

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:54.050
reports as offline, it'll
just stop sending traffic

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:55.019
to that machine.

00:17:55.019 --> 00:17:57.560
So the health check the way the
load balancer thinks about it

00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:59.880
is kind of like a busy signal.

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:02.190
The machine says, I can't
handle any traffic any more.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:02.880
I'm not responding.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:04.338
The load balancer
says, no problem.

00:18:04.338 --> 00:18:05.640
I'll stop sending you traffic.

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:07.830
When the machine comes
back online and starts

00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:09.750
passing its health
checks, the load balancer

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:11.604
will resume sending traffic.

00:18:11.604 --> 00:18:13.020
So that health
check can be fairly

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:14.514
aggressive in
determining machines

00:18:14.514 --> 00:18:16.680
to be unhealthy, because
the cost of going unhealthy

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:19.140
is just, hey, this one machine
temporary stops receiving

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:20.850
traffic and then can resume.

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:23.760
Autohealer is different.

00:18:23.760 --> 00:18:27.300
When a machine goes unhealthy
and it's an autohealing health

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:29.100
check, the autohealer
will recreate

00:18:29.100 --> 00:18:33.480
the machine, which is a much
more disruptive occurrence.

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:35.820
So it does mean that when
you're designing your system,

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:37.440
you want your
autohealer health checks

00:18:37.440 --> 00:18:39.990
to be more conservative
so they only

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:42.780
trigger if the
machine truly is down

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:44.880
and you want to make sure
that it gets recreated

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:46.080
and basically healed.

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:52.640
So now let's put
it all together.

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.890
And let's create a
regional managed instance

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:59.822
group with autohealer and
load balancing and containers.

00:18:59.822 --> 00:19:01.280
So we start with
the health checks.

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:03.570
So let's create two of them.

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:06.780
The first one will be
for the autohealer.

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:08.800
So we'll call it
next-simple-hc-ah.

00:19:12.164 --> 00:19:15.500
And for our little app,
we do the health check

00:19:15.500 --> 00:19:19.425
on /healthcheck, so
it's easy to remember.

00:19:22.859 --> 00:19:25.150
And we'll leave these parameters
as they are basically.

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:27.760
So the check interval is three.

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:30.577
And we can change
the timeout to three.

00:19:30.577 --> 00:19:33.160
And what that means is that the
health check will be triggered

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:34.535
every five seconds
and we'll wait

00:19:34.535 --> 00:19:36.760
three seconds for a response.

00:19:36.760 --> 00:19:39.190
But what we're changing
is the threshold

00:19:39.190 --> 00:19:41.410
to be three as well,
which means that it

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:44.660
takes three fails for the
instance to become unhealthy.

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:46.832
And we're checking
every five seconds.

00:19:46.832 --> 00:19:48.040
So it's a 15 second duration.

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:50.530
So it'll basically
check, wait five seconds,

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:52.710
check, wait five seconds,
check, wait five seconds,

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:54.240
and go OK, you're unhealthy.

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:57.622
But this other health check,
the load balancing one, the LB,

00:19:57.622 --> 00:19:59.320
we use the same path.

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:01.955
So the key is that the
actual health check

00:20:01.955 --> 00:20:04.330
point, like the ping, the
heart beat could be the same.

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:08.660
All we're playing with is the
how aggressive or conservative

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:09.160
it is.

00:20:09.160 --> 00:20:11.090
So this one is a
bit more aggressive.

00:20:11.090 --> 00:20:13.720
So it's every three seconds.

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:16.700
And here the error is
that the timeout is five.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:19.000
So you can't ping
more frequently

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:21.470
than you're waiting for
a response, obviously.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:24.130
So we're going to ping
every three seconds,

00:20:24.130 --> 00:20:27.500
and we're going to just
wait for two responses.

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:29.380
Which means that
it'll take six seconds

00:20:29.380 --> 00:20:34.000
to flag an instance as unhealthy
for load balancing, which

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:36.610
is basically less than half
of our autohealing case.

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:38.770
And that's what I meant,
is that the load balancer

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:40.772
can more quickly say,
hey, I'm not going

00:20:40.772 --> 00:20:41.980
to send you any more traffic.

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:43.688
But the autohealer
will wait a little bit

00:20:43.688 --> 00:20:47.500
longer before it clobbers that
machine and then recreates it.

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:50.530
So now we're creating
the instance group.

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:53.601
We'll call it
next-rmig-containerized-1.

00:20:53.601 --> 00:20:55.600
And to make it containerized,
all you need to do

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:59.860
is toggle this little switch
called make it multi-zone.

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:00.899
Choose us-central.

00:21:00.899 --> 00:21:02.440
Let's just keep it
in the same place.

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:04.356
But now you're selecting
a region, not a zone.

00:21:04.356 --> 00:21:09.970
So it will use the three
zones in that region.

00:21:09.970 --> 00:21:13.930
We'll select a template
called instance-template-v1.

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:16.690
And let's do 12 machines
because, I don't know,

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:19.630
12 divides into 3, so we can
have four machines nicely.

00:21:22.516 --> 00:21:25.520
Hit Create, and there you go.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:27.790
And that's all you needed.

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:31.820
So that regional managed
instance group resource

00:21:31.820 --> 00:21:32.880
is getting created.

00:21:32.880 --> 00:21:34.379
And then as soon
as it becomes live,

00:21:34.379 --> 00:21:36.420
we can start connecting
and networking to it.

00:21:49.137 --> 00:21:49.970
All right, click in.

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:52.032
And the one thing
you'll notice is

00:21:52.032 --> 00:21:53.990
you have that list of
machines getting created,

00:21:53.990 --> 00:21:56.406
but now you can see that they're
split across three zones.

00:21:56.406 --> 00:21:58.922
So you've got the c,
f, and b in there.

00:21:58.922 --> 00:22:00.380
Now we'll go over
to load balancing

00:22:00.380 --> 00:22:01.379
just like we did before.

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:25.190
And edit the back end to
select the newly created

00:22:25.190 --> 00:22:29.497
rmig-containerized-1 regional
manager instance group.

00:22:29.497 --> 00:22:31.080
And that's kind of
all you have to do.

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:34.576
Just select it as a back end
because that's what it is.

00:22:34.576 --> 00:22:35.950
And the difference
from last time

00:22:35.950 --> 00:22:38.304
is now we're going
to select that health

00:22:38.304 --> 00:22:39.220
check that we created.

00:22:39.220 --> 00:22:44.180
So let's find it, next-simple-lb
for load balancing.

00:22:44.180 --> 00:22:46.770
So we want to make sure that
we grab the right health check.

00:22:50.160 --> 00:22:50.660
Hit Update.

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:53.630
And that's it.

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:57.297
And then just make sure that
the instances now got created.

00:22:57.297 --> 00:22:59.630
And the one thing that we
didn't do previously, so let's

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:02.120
fix it now, we didn't actually
select the autohealer health

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:02.290
check.

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:03.456
So let's set up autohealing.

00:23:03.456 --> 00:23:05.599
So all you have to do is
managed instanced group--

00:23:05.599 --> 00:23:07.640
and you can do this at
create or afterwards-- you

00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:09.144
select the health check.

00:23:09.144 --> 00:23:11.060
And then you can also
select an initial delay.

00:23:11.060 --> 00:23:13.310
So that's basically
the amount of time

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:15.410
that the autohealing
will wait before it

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:18.020
starts testing an instance
after the instance gets created.

00:23:18.020 --> 00:23:20.630
So it lets your instance
boot up and become ready

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:23.082
before it starts getting
potentially clobbered.

00:23:23.082 --> 00:23:25.040
So you don't got into
this cycle where it keeps

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:28.520
on autohealing and recreating.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:32.280
So the instances are serving.

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:34.860
So now let's go to
Networking and check

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:38.730
that IP that's
been load balanced.

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:41.220
That's shared amongst
all of the 12 instances

00:23:41.220 --> 00:23:44.530
for receiving traffic.

00:23:44.530 --> 00:23:46.050
Hit Refresh, and there we go.

00:23:46.050 --> 00:23:47.560
And we're serving.

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:48.180
So we're good.

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:49.920
We're up.

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:51.660
So we have that first part down.

00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:55.180
Out of the cycle, we
did that cycle one.

00:23:55.180 --> 00:23:56.620
So we wrote some code.

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:59.050
We built it into a container.

00:23:59.050 --> 00:23:59.650
We tested it.

00:23:59.650 --> 00:24:00.567
We deployed it.

00:24:00.567 --> 00:24:01.150
We're serving.

00:24:01.150 --> 00:24:01.649
We're happy.

00:24:01.649 --> 00:24:02.450
We're good.

00:24:02.450 --> 00:24:04.075
We're ready to go
home for the weekend.

00:24:04.075 --> 00:24:07.010
And then it turns out, hey,
version two dropped in.

00:24:07.010 --> 00:24:09.470
So now you have to update this.

00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:11.220
What are your options?

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:13.880
Well in the past you
had basically two things

00:24:13.880 --> 00:24:16.220
you could do, and both of
them were kind of manual.

00:24:16.220 --> 00:24:18.979
So one of the options is to,
OK, let's do all of this again.

00:24:18.979 --> 00:24:20.645
Let's create a regional
managed instance

00:24:20.645 --> 00:24:21.890
group with version two.

00:24:21.890 --> 00:24:23.270
And version one is serving.

00:24:23.270 --> 00:24:25.520
And now we'll live
reconnect the load balancing

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:28.561
and hope for the best, and then
disconnect the old instance

00:24:28.561 --> 00:24:29.060
group.

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:30.890
And so it's all kind
of you're operating

00:24:30.890 --> 00:24:33.602
on these live instance
groups switching traffic.

00:24:33.602 --> 00:24:35.060
The other option
is maybe you could

00:24:35.060 --> 00:24:37.220
change the template
inside your first group

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:39.260
and then force a recreate
of all the machines.

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:41.780
But then what about
your availability?

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:43.880
As you're recreating
these machines,

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:45.980
you're not serving traffic.

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:48.560
So both of these options
were somewhat workable

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:50.400
but a little bit painful.

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:52.280
So that's why we
introduced the updater.

00:24:55.430 --> 00:24:58.610
Instead of starting with exactly
the details of the updater,

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:00.780
let's just talk
about what it does.

00:25:00.780 --> 00:25:04.110
So at the highest level, the
concept is really simple.

00:25:04.110 --> 00:25:06.600
You have a version one in blue.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:08.422
You want all version to green.

00:25:08.422 --> 00:25:10.380
And you just want to
update your instance group

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:11.838
without having to
touch networking,

00:25:11.838 --> 00:25:14.397
without having to
touch any other setups.

00:25:14.397 --> 00:25:15.730
But you still want that control.

00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:17.670
So let's start
simple and let's just

00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:20.730
do a simple update from
version one to version two.

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:24.630
So we start with our
managed instance group.

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:27.620
And if we look in
the details, we

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:30.150
can see that we're
split across the three

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:31.260
zones, four machines each.

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:34.230
They're all in version
one, so everything's good.

00:25:34.230 --> 00:25:39.290
And now we click this new
button called Rolling Update.

00:25:39.290 --> 00:25:41.450
And we currently
have version one.

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:42.330
Select two.

00:25:42.330 --> 00:25:44.627
That's our green build.

00:25:44.627 --> 00:25:45.710
Don't touch anything else.

00:25:48.220 --> 00:25:49.030
And hit Update.

00:26:07.910 --> 00:26:08.841
And there you go.

00:26:08.841 --> 00:26:10.590
The new version starts
getting rolled out.

00:26:10.590 --> 00:26:13.070
Now you can see that actually
six machines are being touched,

00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:15.111
because what's happening
is one machine per zone,

00:26:15.111 --> 00:26:18.440
so three in total, are getting
destroyed, and three in total

00:26:18.440 --> 00:26:19.320
are getting created.

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:21.260
So we're destroying
three old machines

00:26:21.260 --> 00:26:23.390
and creating three new
machines, one per zone,

00:26:23.390 --> 00:26:24.440
and we're rolling it out.

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:27.861
And in the details you can see
that now we're saying, hey,

00:26:27.861 --> 00:26:30.110
I'm going to take your
template one, version one blue,

00:26:30.110 --> 00:26:33.620
to zero instances, and your
version two up to 12 instances

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:36.830
because you're going from
blue to green, all of them.

00:26:36.830 --> 00:26:39.740
Great, so that works nicely
for that simple update

00:26:39.740 --> 00:26:43.070
where you have to go all
version one to all version two,

00:26:43.070 --> 00:26:46.119
one machine at a
time, or one per zone.

00:26:46.119 --> 00:26:48.410
But real life isn't that
simple because what you really

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:50.100
want to do is this.

00:26:50.100 --> 00:26:52.050
If you have a
service that is live

00:26:52.050 --> 00:26:54.080
and you want it to
be highly available

00:26:54.080 --> 00:26:56.810
and you have a new version and
you probably tested it a lot

00:26:56.810 --> 00:26:59.120
and you're pretty
confident with it,

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:01.430
you still don't want
to roll it out 100%

00:27:01.430 --> 00:27:03.680
into your live production,
just plow it in there

00:27:03.680 --> 00:27:05.212
and hope for the best.

00:27:05.212 --> 00:27:07.670
You probably want to do some
sort of canary, dip your toes,

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:10.344
maybe roll out one machine in
one zone, see how it performs,

00:27:10.344 --> 00:27:12.260
make sure that there's
nothing crazy going on,

00:27:12.260 --> 00:27:13.650
and then go forward from there.

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:17.600
So let's do a simple canary
update with the system, where

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:20.230
we start in the same
place, all versions

00:27:20.230 --> 00:27:25.300
ones, same rmig for per zone.

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:27.920
And let's go to Rolling Update.

00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:31.870
And the difference is, we have
the 12 blue machines in version

00:27:31.870 --> 00:27:32.690
one.

00:27:32.690 --> 00:27:36.450
Let's add one in
the new release.

00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:40.700
So we add a version two, and
we're saying one instance.

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:43.290
And you can see that the
system adjusted the count

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:45.780
to say, well, we're going to
have 11 in the old version

00:27:45.780 --> 00:27:47.050
and one in the new version.

00:27:47.050 --> 00:27:47.883
And then hit Update.

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:03.365
And that's it.

00:28:03.365 --> 00:28:05.740
Whereas last time you saw six
machines were being updated

00:28:05.740 --> 00:28:07.810
because we're going through
all of them, now it's just,

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:09.130
we're creating one and
we're destroying one,

00:28:09.130 --> 00:28:10.152
so we're replacing it.

00:28:10.152 --> 00:28:11.860
And in the details
you can see that we're

00:28:11.860 --> 00:28:14.290
targeting towards 11 and one.

00:28:14.290 --> 00:28:18.340
So the updater is going
to maintain that rollout

00:28:18.340 --> 00:28:20.664
because that's
what you specified.

00:28:20.664 --> 00:28:22.330
So that worked well
for the one machine.

00:28:22.330 --> 00:28:24.550
And let's say you
deployed it and tested it

00:28:24.550 --> 00:28:25.610
and it works well.

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:28.720
So now you want to say,
maybe let's test performance.

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:30.790
But in order to feel
good about performance,

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:32.380
you want to have
a larger rollout.

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:37.330
So let's reload a third, or
33%, into your instance group.

00:28:37.330 --> 00:28:39.970
And you're starting with that
single machine rolled out,

00:28:39.970 --> 00:28:43.120
which is in one of the zones.

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:44.920
And it's serving fine.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:47.710
And when you go to Rolling
Updates, all you have to do

00:28:47.710 --> 00:28:49.469
is say, OK, well instead
of one instance--

00:28:49.469 --> 00:28:51.010
I'm going to think
about percentages,

00:28:51.010 --> 00:28:53.830
because I want to have a third,
whatever the size of my group.

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:56.410
I'll put in 33 for a third.

00:28:56.410 --> 00:28:59.842
And don't touch anything else.

00:28:59.842 --> 00:29:00.980
And hit Update.

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:21.580
And so for that rollout, what
you can see now is the updater

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:23.710
will create the
missing machines.

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:25.810
So it leaves that one
you already updated.

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:27.850
And in the details you
can see that the target

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:31.690
size, what it's going to, is
a third in your new version.

00:29:31.690 --> 00:29:34.840
So it's basically targeting
that 1/3 canary rollout.

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:36.340
And you can check
on this progress

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:38.756
and what the target size is.

00:29:38.756 --> 00:29:41.380
When it finishes the update, you
can run your performance tests

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:44.170
and then either roll
forward or roll back.

00:29:44.170 --> 00:29:45.400
So let's stay focused.

00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:46.510
We're doing really well.

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:48.930
Let's keep going.

00:29:48.930 --> 00:29:51.360
We were skipping over
these parameters.

00:29:51.360 --> 00:29:54.487
And they're actually
that magic sauce

00:29:54.487 --> 00:29:56.070
that makes these
updates configurable.

00:29:56.070 --> 00:29:59.049
So everything we've been doing
so far was along the lines of,

00:29:59.049 --> 00:30:00.840
I just want to get
these machines out there

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:01.950
in a certain percentage.

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:04.300
But there's more to
this than you can do.

00:30:04.300 --> 00:30:06.440
The first thing you
can do is instead

00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:08.940
of rolling out your instances
like we were doing proactively

00:30:08.940 --> 00:30:10.314
where, as soon as
you hit Update,

00:30:10.314 --> 00:30:13.550
they start getting created,
you can be less disruptive.

00:30:13.550 --> 00:30:15.050
You can say, I only
want to roll out

00:30:15.050 --> 00:30:18.070
a new version when the
instance are getting

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:19.820
created for another reason.

00:30:19.820 --> 00:30:23.230
Let's say your instance group
is scaling up or scaling down.

00:30:23.230 --> 00:30:25.300
So that's opportunistic.

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:28.180
So you can say I'm only going
to update when the opportunity

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:30.200
presents itself.

00:30:30.200 --> 00:30:33.370
The second parameter
is maximum surge.

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:36.341
Now this is all
about the concept of,

00:30:36.341 --> 00:30:37.590
where is the update happening?

00:30:37.590 --> 00:30:38.880
It's kind of the scratchpad.

00:30:38.880 --> 00:30:41.730
So if you imagine you have
your serving set of machines,

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:44.800
and you need to take
them to a new version,

00:30:44.800 --> 00:30:47.740
maximum surge allows you to
create a temporary scratchpad

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:50.550
on the side where you can put
up new machines without touching

00:30:50.550 --> 00:30:51.780
these old ones, the
ones that are serving.

00:30:51.780 --> 00:30:53.490
So you put up new
machines, and only

00:30:53.490 --> 00:30:55.770
when they're serving and
confirmed as healthy you

00:30:55.770 --> 00:30:57.060
take away the old ones.

00:30:57.060 --> 00:31:00.040
So it's like a temporary
additional space.

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:03.090
Maximum unavailable is all
about this serving space.

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:04.980
And it's kind of your
disruption budget.

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:07.380
So what you can say
is, I want to update

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:09.060
inside my serving
space, but I only

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:13.164
want to update one machine at
a time or two or three or 50%.

00:31:13.164 --> 00:31:14.580
And so the two
parameters together

00:31:14.580 --> 00:31:17.520
allow you to roll your
updates out either affecting

00:31:17.520 --> 00:31:20.190
your existing
serving space or not.

00:31:20.190 --> 00:31:23.400
And then the final parameter
is maximum wait time,

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.380
which is all about
the rate of the update

00:31:25.380 --> 00:31:27.810
or how much testing
you want to do.

00:31:27.810 --> 00:31:29.910
So if you want to,
for example, not just

00:31:29.910 --> 00:31:32.040
go one machine after the
other with a zero wait

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:34.000
time, which is what
we've been doing so far,

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:36.450
but you want to say I
want to, for example,

00:31:36.450 --> 00:31:39.360
pause for 10 minutes between
each machines because I want

00:31:39.360 --> 00:31:42.619
to have a really gradual one
machine at a time slow update,

00:31:42.619 --> 00:31:44.910
then you would just put in,
let's say, 10 minutes here.

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:48.570
And after a machine updates
and passes its health checks,

00:31:48.570 --> 00:31:52.690
the next one won't start
for that wait time.

00:31:52.690 --> 00:31:54.510
So with all of
these together, you

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:57.400
can really configure the update
exactly how you want it to be.

00:31:57.400 --> 00:32:00.890
And so for example one of the
opposite ways from safety,

00:32:00.890 --> 00:32:03.510
because everything we did so
far was this idea of a really

00:32:03.510 --> 00:32:04.800
slow and safe update.

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:07.770
What if you have a critical
fix or a fast rollback

00:32:07.770 --> 00:32:10.080
and you want to update
as quickly as possible,

00:32:10.080 --> 00:32:12.480
but you still don't want
to affect your serving set?

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:16.410
Then just set you
max surge to 100%,

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:18.335
and set your
unavailable to zero.

00:32:18.335 --> 00:32:19.710
And then what the
updater will do

00:32:19.710 --> 00:32:23.550
will spin up duplicate
machines in the new version,

00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:26.475
make sure they're healthy, and
then destroy the old machines

00:32:26.475 --> 00:32:28.350
only after these ones
pass the health checks.

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:31.080
So it'll do an update
basically in one step,

00:32:31.080 --> 00:32:34.290
even if it is like dozens
or hundreds of machines.

00:32:34.290 --> 00:32:37.060
And it's a way to
basically not have

00:32:37.060 --> 00:32:39.020
to wait for that kind
of rolling update.

00:32:39.020 --> 00:32:42.530
So with these
parameters altogether,

00:32:42.530 --> 00:32:46.834
you can get different effects.

00:32:46.834 --> 00:32:49.770
Can I switch to the
computer please?

00:32:49.770 --> 00:32:52.100
So for the last
update, I'm just going

00:32:52.100 --> 00:32:55.010
to show the actual
interface just

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:59.337
to show that it is working.

00:32:59.337 --> 00:33:01.170
Let's say I want to
plow through version two

00:33:01.170 --> 00:33:02.460
as quickly as possible.

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:07.210
And then we change the
instances to percent.

00:33:07.210 --> 00:33:11.350
And so that will be all of
the instances that are going

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:13.190
to be surging, so additional.

00:33:13.190 --> 00:33:14.560
No instances unavailable.

00:33:14.560 --> 00:33:17.380
So I don't want to touch
my serving instances.

00:33:17.380 --> 00:33:18.535
I don't want to wait.

00:33:18.535 --> 00:33:19.330
Hit Update.

00:33:35.950 --> 00:33:38.900
And what you can see is that
all of these new machines

00:33:38.900 --> 00:33:41.180
in version two are spinning
up at the same time,

00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:43.400
but the version ones
are not being touched.

00:33:43.400 --> 00:33:46.460
And the other important thing is
if you tab over to the details,

00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:48.594
you actually have 24
machines out of the 12.

00:33:48.594 --> 00:33:50.510
So we doubled up our
capacity, which obviously

00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:52.343
means that you have to
have the quota for it

00:33:52.343 --> 00:33:54.237
and you incur the extra costs.

00:33:54.237 --> 00:33:55.070
But it is temporary.

00:33:55.070 --> 00:33:57.236
It's only for as long as
it takes for these machines

00:33:57.236 --> 00:33:59.910
to spin up and pass
their health checks.

00:33:59.910 --> 00:34:02.840
So it might be worth
it in certain cases.

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:04.820
And in terms of
the training, we're

00:34:04.820 --> 00:34:09.120
going to none of these
and all of these.

00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:10.600
OK, can I go back please?

00:34:13.810 --> 00:34:16.502
One more concept with the
thinking about updates

00:34:16.502 --> 00:34:17.710
that's going to be important.

00:34:17.710 --> 00:34:20.920
So when we were talking
about the 1/3 rollout,

00:34:20.920 --> 00:34:23.500
because the managed instance
group could have autoscaling,

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:25.030
you want that 1/3
to be maintained

00:34:25.030 --> 00:34:26.030
as you're scaling up.

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:28.230
So that's an important
consideration where

00:34:28.230 --> 00:34:30.010
an update isn't an action.

00:34:30.010 --> 00:34:33.340
That's basically it you
set it and you're done.

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:35.267
An update is a
target, because if you

00:34:35.267 --> 00:34:36.850
want to have a third
rollout, then you

00:34:36.850 --> 00:34:39.219
want that third to be
maintained as you're scaling up

00:34:39.219 --> 00:34:40.219
and you're scaling down.

00:34:40.219 --> 00:34:42.610
So in this case, if three
instances are getting added,

00:34:42.610 --> 00:34:44.026
two of them will
be in one version

00:34:44.026 --> 00:34:45.820
and 1/3 will be in
the other version.

00:34:49.555 --> 00:34:50.679
That was a lot of updating.

00:34:50.679 --> 00:34:55.370
So let's think about
one more thing.

00:34:55.370 --> 00:34:57.370
We talked about containers
as a packaging thing.

00:34:57.370 --> 00:34:59.286
That's what we started
our little example with

00:34:59.286 --> 00:35:00.670
and that's what we worked with.

00:35:00.670 --> 00:35:02.240
And we talked about
templates and changing them.

00:35:02.240 --> 00:35:03.073
And that's all good.

00:35:03.073 --> 00:35:05.554
But if you're using, let's
say, startup scripts,

00:35:05.554 --> 00:35:07.220
you don't need to
worry about templates.

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:08.219
So none of this applies.

00:35:08.219 --> 00:35:10.702
And if you are using, let's
say, baked images and you

00:35:10.702 --> 00:35:12.160
have an image family
where you just

00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:13.618
want to be recreating
your machines

00:35:13.618 --> 00:35:16.600
and not touching the template
and you pulling new images,

00:35:16.600 --> 00:35:18.530
again, that's not what you want.

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:21.670
So there is also a
restart recreate command

00:35:21.670 --> 00:35:25.210
where it's the same concept,
same parameters in terms

00:35:25.210 --> 00:35:27.760
of control with max
unavailable, minimal wait time.

00:35:27.760 --> 00:35:29.770
But you can just restart
or recreate machines

00:35:29.770 --> 00:35:33.160
without touching templates.

00:35:33.160 --> 00:35:35.130
So I started this whole
thing with the idea

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:38.570
of managed and integrated
features working together.

00:35:38.570 --> 00:35:40.320
And now you might be
thinking, so OK good.

00:35:40.320 --> 00:35:41.778
So you've got all
these automations

00:35:41.778 --> 00:35:43.380
and they're all doing
their own thing.

00:35:43.380 --> 00:35:45.000
How do they interact?

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:47.370
We touched a little bit upon
the autoscaling updating,

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:50.100
but there's more
detail than this.

00:35:50.100 --> 00:35:52.692
What about, let's say,
autohealer and updater?

00:35:52.692 --> 00:35:56.067
I talked about the disruption
budget for the updater.

00:35:56.067 --> 00:35:57.400
And then there's the autohealer.

00:35:57.400 --> 00:35:59.066
So what happens if
you launch an update,

00:35:59.066 --> 00:36:01.450
and let's say you set your
destruction budget to five,

00:36:01.450 --> 00:36:03.272
because you can
only afford to have

00:36:03.272 --> 00:36:04.480
five machines down at a time.

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:06.460
And you set your max
unavailable to five.

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:10.370
And then the autohealer comes
in and kills three machines.

00:36:10.370 --> 00:36:12.040
Well the updater
will know about this.

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:15.280
So it will only use
two machines while this

00:36:15.280 --> 00:36:18.620
is happening, because it
understands that three are down

00:36:18.620 --> 00:36:20.680
for some other reason.

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:22.990
When they get healed up
and come back online,

00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:24.890
the updater will
pick them up and now

00:36:24.890 --> 00:36:27.054
have its full budget
of five to work with.

00:36:27.054 --> 00:36:28.220
So that works really nicely.

00:36:28.220 --> 00:36:29.920
So you don't have to
mess with the autohealer

00:36:29.920 --> 00:36:31.330
if you want to
roll out an update.

00:36:31.330 --> 00:36:32.788
And you can still
have your budget.

00:36:32.788 --> 00:36:34.660
But what about the autoscaler?

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:36.340
This is a little bit trickier.

00:36:36.340 --> 00:36:38.990
If you have an autoscaler and
you set it to a certain target

00:36:38.990 --> 00:36:42.130
threshold, and it figured out
that it needs five machines

00:36:42.130 --> 00:36:44.177
to serve this traffic,
and then you say,

00:36:44.177 --> 00:36:45.760
OK, I'm going to
roll out a new update

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:47.650
and I'm going to take
some machines down.

00:36:47.650 --> 00:36:50.476
So two machines
disappear for update.

00:36:50.476 --> 00:36:51.850
That traffic has
to go somewhere,

00:36:51.850 --> 00:36:54.370
and it'll be served by the
remaining three machines.

00:36:54.370 --> 00:36:57.520
But of course now they're
well over their target load.

00:36:57.520 --> 00:36:58.937
And so you might
ask the question,

00:36:58.937 --> 00:37:00.561
will the autoscaler
freak out and start

00:37:00.561 --> 00:37:01.540
adding these machines?

00:37:01.540 --> 00:37:03.340
Well no because the
autoscaler knows

00:37:03.340 --> 00:37:05.230
that those two machines
are down for updates

00:37:05.230 --> 00:37:06.479
and they're coming right back.

00:37:06.479 --> 00:37:10.120
So as soon as they show up,
they'll pick up the traffic.

00:37:10.120 --> 00:37:12.580
And the autoscaler doesn't
need to scale because it knows

00:37:12.580 --> 00:37:14.119
that it actually wants five.

00:37:14.119 --> 00:37:15.910
Now if in the middle
of the update actually

00:37:15.910 --> 00:37:18.490
there was more traffic and
suddenly the autoscaler

00:37:18.490 --> 00:37:20.300
thought, hey, I
need six machines,

00:37:20.300 --> 00:37:22.050
then it would spin up
additional machines.

00:37:22.050 --> 00:37:23.350
So it's not like it's paused.

00:37:23.350 --> 00:37:25.480
It's just including
what's happening

00:37:25.480 --> 00:37:27.500
in its overall calculations.

00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:31.090
So that's the idea of it
being an integrated system.

00:37:31.090 --> 00:37:33.070
And of course all
of the examples

00:37:33.070 --> 00:37:35.601
I gave you were with the UI
because it's kind of more fun

00:37:35.601 --> 00:37:36.100
to show.

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:38.230
But it's fully
supported by gcloud,

00:37:38.230 --> 00:37:41.320
so you can write scripts and use
all these parameters in gcloud

00:37:41.320 --> 00:37:42.782
as well.

00:37:42.782 --> 00:37:43.990
So let's put it all together.

00:37:43.990 --> 00:37:46.750
We talked about managed.

00:37:46.750 --> 00:37:50.020
And now you can set up a
regional managed instance

00:37:50.020 --> 00:37:52.217
group, which is resistant
to traffic changes

00:37:52.217 --> 00:37:54.550
with autoscaling because you
can add and remove machines

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:56.560
based on whatever
load you specify

00:37:56.560 --> 00:37:58.570
or however you think about load.

00:37:58.570 --> 00:38:03.430
You can be resilient to machines
crashing with autohealing.

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:07.510
You can take new versions
and roll out updates.

00:38:07.510 --> 00:38:12.200
And you can also be resistant
to zonal outages with regional.

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:14.420
So now I wanted to talk to
you or share with you how

00:38:14.420 --> 00:38:15.980
one of our customers,
Descartes Labs,

00:38:15.980 --> 00:38:17.730
used all of these
features together in two

00:38:17.730 --> 00:38:19.670
of their specific use cases.

00:38:19.670 --> 00:38:22.010
Now if you don't know
who Descartes is,

00:38:22.010 --> 00:38:23.960
they're a machine
learning specialist.

00:38:23.960 --> 00:38:27.050
And one of their big products
is crop yield predictions

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:30.630
and famine predictions
based on satellite imagery.

00:38:30.630 --> 00:38:32.930
Now like all machine learning,
what they do this with

00:38:32.930 --> 00:38:34.820
is, unsurprisingly,
a ton of computation.

00:38:34.820 --> 00:38:37.190
And in fact they run
tens and thousands

00:38:37.190 --> 00:38:39.710
of cores inside MIGs
all day every day

00:38:39.710 --> 00:38:42.686
to create these models.

00:38:42.686 --> 00:38:44.560
In practice that means
they actually ended up

00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:47.116
creating a managed instance
group with a thousand machines.

00:38:47.116 --> 00:38:49.240
And all of these machines
are pretty beefy to chunk

00:38:49.240 --> 00:38:51.400
through the machine
learning models.

00:38:51.400 --> 00:38:54.820
But the tricky thing is
when a new version shows up,

00:38:54.820 --> 00:38:56.860
they don't care about
the old version at all

00:38:56.860 --> 00:38:59.972
because there's no point
running the old code when

00:38:59.972 --> 00:39:01.180
you want to have a new model.

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:04.240
So when they're rolling
out their update,

00:39:04.240 --> 00:39:05.590
their disruption budget is 100%.

00:39:05.590 --> 00:39:06.280
This isn't serving.

00:39:06.280 --> 00:39:08.405
It's basically a batch job
that's just continuously

00:39:08.405 --> 00:39:09.860
running on new batches of data.

00:39:09.860 --> 00:39:12.770
So when you want to
roll out a new version,

00:39:12.770 --> 00:39:14.230
you can do this all at once.

00:39:14.230 --> 00:39:17.080
And when they run this,
basically all the machines

00:39:17.080 --> 00:39:18.770
get updated essentially
in one step,

00:39:18.770 --> 00:39:20.520
even though it's like
a thousand machines.

00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:22.960
So it's a really
fast parallel update.

00:39:22.960 --> 00:39:26.920
Now their other use case is a
little bit more traditional.

00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:29.140
Once these models get
created, they actually

00:39:29.140 --> 00:39:30.790
have to get served
to their customers.

00:39:30.790 --> 00:39:33.520
And they do this through
custom front ends.

00:39:33.520 --> 00:39:37.150
And these front ends
translate that data

00:39:37.150 --> 00:39:38.470
in a way that's human readable.

00:39:38.470 --> 00:39:40.094
And their customers
can use these.

00:39:40.094 --> 00:39:42.010
Now they actually have
17 of these front ends.

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:45.340
Each one is a MIG and it's
autoscaled from one to 100.

00:39:45.340 --> 00:39:48.370
And when a new version comes
out, all they need to do

00:39:48.370 --> 00:39:50.080
is just actually
restart the machines,

00:39:50.080 --> 00:39:56.026
because they use startup scripts
for their actual software.

00:39:56.026 --> 00:39:57.400
But because they're
serving, they

00:39:57.400 --> 00:39:58.660
can't take them down fully.

00:39:58.660 --> 00:40:01.030
So in this case, based on--

00:40:05.481 --> 00:40:07.730
in this case when they're
rolling out the new version,

00:40:07.730 --> 00:40:13.920
all they need to do is restart
with a 10% disruption budget,

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:17.850
because based on
their use case, that's

00:40:17.850 --> 00:40:19.290
not noticeable by customers.

00:40:19.290 --> 00:40:22.676
But it's also fast enough to
go through all of the machines.

00:40:22.676 --> 00:40:24.300
And because there
are 17 of these MIGs,

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:28.110
they can do a scripted rollout
with gcloud commands going one

00:40:28.110 --> 00:40:28.890
at a time.

00:40:28.890 --> 00:40:31.260
But because they're
nonblocking, all 17

00:40:31.260 --> 00:40:33.010
gets submitted basically
at the same time.

00:40:33.010 --> 00:40:37.744
And the MIGs update or
restart in parallel.

00:40:37.744 --> 00:40:40.160
Now if you want to actually
know more about Descartes Labs

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:41.690
and how they set
up their systems,

00:40:41.690 --> 00:40:43.250
there is a great
talk they did today,

00:40:43.250 --> 00:40:49.430
IO235, how Descartes Labs
uses Postgres on GCP.

00:40:49.430 --> 00:40:51.722
And they also talk a little
bit more about their setup.

00:40:51.722 --> 00:40:53.221
So it's worth
checking out if you're

00:40:53.221 --> 00:40:54.450
interested in that use case.

00:40:57.230 --> 00:40:59.330
So there you have it.

00:40:59.330 --> 00:41:03.720
We started with containers
and easy container deployments

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:06.750
on single machines or
directly into MIGs.

00:41:06.750 --> 00:41:10.140
We looked at regional manager
instance groups, which

00:41:10.140 --> 00:41:12.480
now can be split
into zones, have

00:41:12.480 --> 00:41:15.450
autohealing,
autoscaling, updates.

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:17.790
And for updates, we looked
at the new updater features

00:41:17.790 --> 00:41:19.740
which give you a lot of
flexibility and power

00:41:19.740 --> 00:41:23.070
with how you version or
roll out new versions

00:41:23.070 --> 00:41:30.151
of your software to your MIGs.

00:41:30.151 --> 00:41:32.150
If you want to get started
or want to know more,

00:41:32.150 --> 00:41:33.620
there's plenty of documentation.

00:41:33.620 --> 00:41:35.286
And I think these
slides will be shared.

00:41:35.286 --> 00:41:37.810
So if you didn't take
great notes, no worries.

00:41:37.810 --> 00:41:40.130
And I just want to leave
you with this final thought,

00:41:40.130 --> 00:41:42.610
to bring it all together.

00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:45.460
Even if you want that kind of
direct control of instances

00:41:45.460 --> 00:41:47.830
for your infrastructure,
you don't

00:41:47.830 --> 00:41:49.540
have to do everything by hand.

00:41:49.540 --> 00:41:51.550
Build the service by
choosing the managed

00:41:51.550 --> 00:41:54.340
features that work for you.

00:41:54.340 --> 00:41:59.750
And we'll help you out with
management of your service.

00:41:59.750 --> 00:42:01.610
You're not on your own.

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:02.990
Thank you very much.

00:42:02.990 --> 00:42:05.420
I think we've got some
time for questions as well.

00:42:05.420 --> 00:42:07.820
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:13.270
